Count what ANGLE holds in Metal, by class, for an embedder's memory line (Minato, patch 0015).

Why: an app's process on the iPhone held about 2.3 GB of memory tagged as graphics and 1.6 GB more that no ledger
named, and nothing said which GL objects held it. Metal resources in private storage (depth/stencil, multisample
render targets) count outside the graphics ledger. Every texture and buffer the context's device makes now gets an
associated object that counts it by class (compressed, color, depth/stencil, multisample, memoryless textures;
shared and private buffers) with its allocatedSize until Metal frees it, so a resource a command buffer still holds
after its delete counts until it is really gone. The buffer manager's free list and the device's
currentAllocatedSize come with it. ANGLEMetalMemoryCounters gives the values, ANGLEMetalMemoryLine one line
without allocating.
Test: graphics/tests/angle_memory_counters.mm (an RGBA8 and an ETC2 texture, a DEPTH24_STENCIL8 and a 4x
renderbuffer and a buffer each count in their class, the renderbuffers as private, and the texture classes come back
after the deletes).
Upstream: not planned (an embedder's diagnostic).
